g Denmark’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/103775"} "Jeanette Ottesen"] " to the wall, as the Dane was 0.66 seconds under the world record split, with Vollmer only in third place. But the last lap was all Vollmer as she came home in a world record 55.98 to become the first female swimmer to better 56 seconds in the 100 fly. Ottesen’s start was too fast and she faded to sixth place, as the silver was won by China’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/122335"} "Lu Ying"] ", bronze medalist at the 2011 Worlds. The bronze went to Australian " [:a {:href "/athletes/115744"} "Alicia Coutts"] ", runner-up to Vollmer at the 2011 Worlds, who inexplicably was in last place at the turn, but stormed home to win a medal."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/103470"} "Dana Vollmer"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "56.25 (1 h6)"] [:td {} "56.36 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "55.98 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/122335"} "Lu Ying"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CHN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/CHN.png"}] "CHN"]] [:td {} "57.17 (2 h6)"] [:td {} "57.51 (2 h1)"] [:td {} "56.87 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/115744"} "Alicia Coutts"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/AUS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/AUS.png"}] "AUS"]] [:td {} "57.36 (1 h4)"]